201:
** ἀκρο-ᾱτήριον**, τό,
**place of audience,** {{NT|NT.Acts.25.23}}; **lecture-room**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.528 (_plural_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.45f, etc.}}
**II** **audience**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cat.Ma.” 22.}}

202:
** ἀκρο-ᾱτής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**hearer**, of persons who come to hear a public speaker, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 536c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 286, etc.}}; **disciple, pupil**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1274a29, compare “EN” 1095a2.}}
**II** **reader**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 1, “Lys.” 12.}}

203:
** ἀκρο-βυστία**, ἡ,
**foreskin**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.17.11, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus “Fragmenta” 49 H., NT.Act.11.3.}}
**II** **state of having the foreskin, uncircumcision,** {{NT|NT.Rom.2.25, etc.}}
**II.2** _collective_, **the uncircumcised**, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.26, 3.30, etc. (Prob. from ἄκρος and a Semitic root, cf. Bab.}}**buśtu** 'pudenda', Heb. **bōsheth** 'shame': wrongly derived from ἄκρος, βύω by {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 53.48.)}}

206:
** ἄκρον**, ου, τό,
(_neuter_ of ἄκρος) like{ἄκρα}, **highest** or **farthest point**:
**1** **mountain top, peak**, Γάργαρον ἄκρον Ἴδης{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.292; ἄκρον ὑπερβαλέειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.597}}; τὰ ἄκρα **heights**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.100, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 110e, etc.}}
**1.b** ἄκρα νάων ships' **tops**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 12.9.}}
**2** **headland, cape**, Σούνιον ἄκρον Ἀθηνέων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.278.}}
**3** **end, extremity**, τὰ ἄ. τῆς θαλάσσης, [τοῦἀέρος], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109d, 109e}}; ἄκρα χειρῶν **hands**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 6}}; ἐξ ἄκρων at **the end**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 29; ἐξ ἄκρου “Comica Adespota” 398; ἐπ᾽ ἄκροις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 220d}}:—**border, frontier**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.42.2.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **highest pitch, height**, πανδοξίας ἄκρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.11}}; εἰς ἄκρον ἀνδρείας ἱκέσθαι to **highest pitch**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 58}}; εἰς ἄκρον ἁδύς **exceedingly**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.61}}; ἐπ᾽ ἄκρον ἀφικέσθαι, ἐλθεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 268e, “Ti.” 20a; πρὸς ἄκρῳ γενέσθαι [prev. author] “Phdr.” 247b; ἄκρον ἔχων σοφίης “epigram Gr.” 442 (from Nabataea)}}; ἄκρον ἐρώτων εἰδότος, ἄκρα μάχας{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.448 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus):}} —ἄκρα, τά, **heights, highest point**, οὔτοι ποθ᾽ ἥξει (sic) τῶν ἄκρων ἄνευ πόνου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 397}}; ἄκρα φέρεσθαι win **prize**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 12.31; ἄκρα φέρουσ᾽ ἀρετῆς ὑμῖν “epigram Gr.” 224.2 (from Samos).}}
**II.2** of persons, Ἄργεος ἄκρα Πελασγοί **pride** of Argos, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.142.}}
**III** δρυὸς ἄκρα, ={ἀκρόδρυα}, {{Refs|[prev. work] 112.}}
**IV** in Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus τὰ ἄκρα}} are **major and minor terms** of syllogism, opposed to to μέσον or middle, {{Refs|“APr.” 25b36, +others}}
**V** **extremes** in a proportion, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1133b2.}}

208:
** ἀκῡρόω**,
**cancel, set aside**, ψήφισμα, δόγμα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.63, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.24; ὀφειλήματα “SIG” 742.30 (_passive_, Ephesus),compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.4.10}} (_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.72, “BGU” 1053 ii 14 (1st c.BC).}}
**2** **set at naught, treat as of no effect**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.1Esd.6.32; λόγον θεοῦ NT.Matt.15.6, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.8.8.}}
**2.b** **reject, deny the validity of**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 30.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **render powerless**, τῷ λογισμῷ τὸν τῶν παθῶν οἶστρον{{LXX|LXX.4Mac.2.3.}}

210:
** ἀέκων**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_; _Attic dialect_ and Trag. _contraction_ ἄκων [ᾱ], ουσα, ον (un_contraction_ form also in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 1.61 (law of Draco), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 39 (_anapaest meter_)}}, sometimes found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, as 4.120, 164)}}:— **involuntary, constrained**, of persons, ἀέκοντος ἐμεῖο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.310; ἑκὼν ἀέκοντί γε θυμῷ 4.43; πόλλ᾽ ἀέκων 11.557}}; opposed to βουλόμενος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 11}}; τὼ δ᾽ οὐκ ἀέκοντε πετέσθην (variant{ἄκοντε}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.366, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.484; κάρτα ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.111}}; ἀέκουσι (variant{ἀεκούσια}) δάκρυα παραρρεῖ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.19}}: _contraction_ first in {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 413}}; ἄκοντος Διός **invito Jove**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 771; repeated, ἄκοντά σ᾽ ἄκων προσπασσαλεύσω [prev. work] 19, compare 671; ἄ. ἀκούειν οὓς ἑκὼν εἶπεν λόγους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 929, compare “Ant.” 276; μηδένα τῶνδ᾽ ἀέκοντα μένειν κατέρυκε 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 467.}}_adverb_ ἀκόντως **unwillingly**, ὁμολογεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 333b, compare “Hp.Mi.” 374d; οὐκ ἀ. ἀλλὰ προθύμως ἐπείσθησαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.5.}}
**II** poetical, like{ἀκούσιος}, of acts or their consequences, **involuntary**, κακὰ ἑκόντα κοὐκ ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1230; ἔργων ἀ. [prev. author] “OC” 240 (Lyric poetry), compare 977.}}

213:
** ἀλαζ-ών** [ᾰλ], όνος, ὁ, ἡ,
(ἄλη) properly **wanderer about country, vagrant**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus Comedy texts 31.}}
**II** **charlatan, quack**, especially of Sophists, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 380, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 102, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 173c, +others}}
**II.2** **braggart, boaster**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.12, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1127a21}}; title of play by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus}}
**II.3** _adjective_, **boastful, pretentious**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12; ἀ. λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 560c}}: _comparative_ **-έστερος**[{{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {εἴρων}: _superlative_, ἡδονὴ ἀλαζονίστατον}}] **most shameless**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 65c}}. _adverb_ _superlative_ -έστατα, δρω-ν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.29.}}

214:
** ἀλᾰλ-άζω**,
_future_ -άξομαι variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 593}}, **-άξω**{{LXX|LXX.Eze.27.30}}: _aorist_ ἠλάλαξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 981, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus (see. below), poetry ἀλάλαξα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.37}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 534, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.10.3: (formed from the cry ἀλαλαί)}}: —**raise the war-cry**, τῷ Ἐνυαλίῳ ἠλάλαξαν (as variant for{ἠλέλιξαν}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14, compare 6.5.27}}; _middle_, Arr.[same place]: with _accusative_ _cognate_, νίκην ἀλαλάζειν **shout the shout of** victory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 133.}}
**2** generally, **cry, shout aloud**, Pi.[same place], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 855}}; especially in orgiastic rites, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 57}}; of Bacchus and Bacchae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 593 (in _middle_), 1133, etc.}}; ὠλόλυξαν αἱ γυναῖκες, ἠλάλαξαν δὲ οἱ ἄνδρες{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 3.5.}}
**3** rarely of a cry of pain or grief, ἠλάλαζε δυσθνἥσκων φόνὡ{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 843, LXX.Jer.4.8, +others, NT.Mark.5.38, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Luc” 28.}}
**II** rarely also of other sounds than the voice, **sound loudly**, ψαλμὸς δ᾽ ἀλαλάζει{{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 57; κύμβαλον ἀλαλάζον NT.1Cor.13.1.—poetical word, used by 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus and in late Prose.}}

215:
** ἀλάλητος** [λᾰ], ον,
**unspeakable, unutterable**, variant in {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 422, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 5.3 (1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus), NT.Rom.8.26.}}_adverb_ -τως, as explanation of ἀλόγως{{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 723.30.}}
**II** ἀλάλητα· ξύλα ποταμόκλυστα, γομφώδη, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

218:
** ἀλείφω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.8, etc.}}: _future_ **-ψω**{{LXX|LXX.Exo.40.15}}, (ἐξ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1486, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 386c}}: _aorist_ ἤλειψα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, _Attic dialect_, _Epic dialect_ ἄλειψα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.177:}}_perfect_ ἀλήλῐφα (ἀπ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.2)}}:—_middle_, _future_ **-ψομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.68:}}_aorist_ ἠλειψάμην _Attic dialect_, _Epic dialect_ ἀλ-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.171:}} —_passive_, _future_ ἀλειφθήσομαι (ἐξ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.73}}: _aorist 1_ ἠλείφθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 217c, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ ἐξ-ηλίφην variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258b}}, (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 55.3: pf ἀλήλιμμαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.68}}, (ἐξ-, ὑπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.6 (-ει- is frequently found in _perfect_ forms in codices): (ἀ-, _euphonic_, λιπ-, compare λίπος)}}:—**anoint the skin with oil**, as was done after bathing, _active_ referring to another, _middle_ to oneself, λοῦσαι κέλετ᾽ ἀμφί τ᾽ ἀλεῖψαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.582; 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus elsewhere always adds λίπα or λίπ᾽ ἐλαίῳ (see. entry λίπα), πάντα λοέσσατο καὶ λίπ᾽ ἄλειψεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.227; λοεσσαμένω καὶ ἀλειψαμένω λίπ᾽ ἐλαίῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.577, compare 14.171, 18.350}}: later of **anointing for gymnastic exercises**, λίπα μετὰ τοῦ γυμνάζεσθαι ἠλείψαντο{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6; generally, λίπα ἀλείφεσθαι [prev. author] 4.68; βακκάρι ῥῖνας 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 41}}; of **anointing** the sick, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 60, cf. NT.Jam.5.14.}}
**2** **supply oil** for gymnasts, ἀλειφούσης τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” (add.) (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus)}}; ἀ. πανήγυριν, ἔθνη, {{Refs|“Inscription Magn.” 163, “OGI” 533.47 (from Ancyra)}}; οἱ -όμενοι **youths undergoing gymnastic training**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 339.72 (from Sestos), etc.}}; οἱ ἀ. ἐν τῷ γυμνασίῳ {{Refs|[prev. work]764.5 (Pergam.), +others}}; ἀλείφεσθαι παρά τινι **to attend** a **gymnastic school**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.2.26.}}
**3** **polish**, τράπεζαν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 74; δακτύλιον 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 21; ἀγάλματα 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.33.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, **prepare as if for gymnastics, encourage, stimulate, instigate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.6; ἐπὶ τὴν πολιτικὴν ἀγωνίαν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.59 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; τινὰ ἐπὶ τὸν Κλώδιον 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.16, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 3; τινὰ κατά τινος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.549; τινὰ ἐπὶ φαρμακείαν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μακεδονική” 11.7}}:—_passive_, τοὺς -ομένους ἐπί τι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.158 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II** **daub, plaster, besmear**, οὔατα ἀλεῖψαι **stop up** ears, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.47, 177, 200; ἀ. αἵματι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.8; μίλτῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.5; ψιμυθίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 217d; κυανῷ 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.11.5.}}

220:
** ἀλέκτωρ**, ορος, ἡ,
(ἀ- _privative_, λέγω)
**B** ={ἄλεκτρος}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.08b.}}

225:
** ἀλήθ-εια** [ᾰλ], ἡ,
_Doric dialect_ ἀλάθεια (also ἀλαθείᾱ{{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.204); ἀλάθεα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 57, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.1 is _neuter_ _plural_ of ἀλᾱθή; _Epic dialect_ (and Farly _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to Hdn.Gr. 2.454)}} ἀληθεί; _Ionic dialect_ ἀληθείη:
**I** **truth**, opposed to **lie** or **mere appearance**:
**I.1** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only opposed to **a lie**, frequently in phrase ἀληθείην καταλέξαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.407, +others; ἀ. ἀποειπεῖν 23.361}}; παιδὸς πᾶσαν ἀ. μυθεῖσθαι to tell whole **truth** about the lad, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.507; ἀλάθει᾽ ἀτρεκής 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.17, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus [same place]}}; proverbial, οἶνος καὶ ἀ. `**in vino veritas'**, Alc.[same place], etc.; ἁπλᾶ γάρ ἐστι τῆς ἀ. ἔπη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 176, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 469; χρᾶσθαι τῇ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; εἰπεῖν τὴν ἀ. [prev. author] 6.69; ἡ ἀ. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.122, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 91}}; ἀ. ἔχειν to be **true**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281a42: _plural_, ταῖς ἀ. χρῆσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.5; τὰς ἀ. λέγειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 87, 925; τὰς ἀ. ἀκοῦσαι τῶν γενομένων 4th c.BC: Alcidamas Rhetor “Ὀδυσσεύς.” 13:—Ἀλήθεια or περὶ Ἀληθείας, title of works by 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161c, “Cra.” 391c; by 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista, “FOxy.” 1364, compare 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.11, etc.}}
**I.2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} also **truth, reality**, opposed to **appearance**, σὺν ἀλαθείᾳ καλῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.96; ἡ ἀ. τῶν πραχθέντων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.1; τῶν ἔργων ἡ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41; μιμήματα ἀληθείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c:}} — in _adverbial_ usages, τῇ ἀ. **in very truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.120, etc.; ταῖς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.283, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 130, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.40.5, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 75.20; rarely (without the _Article_) ἀληθείᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343d:—with Preps., ἐν τῇ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 183d}}; ἐπὶ τῆς ἀληθείας καὶ τοῦ πράγματος **in truth and reality**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.72}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀληθείᾳ **for the sake of truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 628, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 891}}; also, **according to truth and nature**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.44:— μετ᾽ ἀληθείας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.4:— κατὰ τὴν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.2.46, etc.; κατ᾽ ἀλήθειον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278b33, etc.:— ξὺν ἀληθείᾳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1567:— πρὸς ἀλήθειαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.67, etc.}}
**I.3** **real war**, opposed to **exercise** or **parade**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.20.4, +others; ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς τῆς ἀ. [prev. author] 1.21.3.}}
**I.4** **true event, realization** of dream or omen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.64, Damon cited in Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1003.}}
**II** of persons, **truthfulness, sincerity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.55; ἀλαθείᾳ φρενῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1550, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1108a20.}}
**III** *)a. personified, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 1, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.29, etc.}}
**IV** **symbol of truth, jewel** worn by Egyptian high-priest, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.48, 75, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 14.34}}: of the Thummim, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.8.8.}}

226:
** ἀληθ-εύω**,
_future_ **-εύσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.5, +others}}:—**speak truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 562, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 589c; περί τι [prev. author] “Tht.” 202b: with _neuter_}}_adjective_, ἀ. πάντα **speak truth** in all things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 14; πολλὰ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.15}}; τὰς δέκα ἡμέρας ἠλήθευσε he **rightly foretold.. **, {{Refs|[prev. work] 5.6.18}}; ἀ. τοὺς ἐπαίνους **prove** their praises **true**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 20}}; τοὔνομα `make good', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 1.4c.}}
**2** of things, **to be, prove true**, σημεῖα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 25}}:—_passive_, **to be fulfilled**, of conditions, ἐπὶ τούτοις -ομένοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.10, frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}:—_active_ of reasoners, **arrive at truth**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1062a25}}:—_passive_, ὁ λόγος -εύεται **is in accordance with truth, Top.** {{Refs|132b4, +others}}; ἀληθεύεσθαι κατά τινος **to be truly predicated of**.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 132a31, +others:}}_future_ _middle_ in same sense, {{Refs|“EN” 1100a35, +others}}

227:
** ἀληθ-ής** [ᾰ],
_Doric dialect_ ἀλᾱθής, ές, (λήθω, ={λανθάνω}: ἀληθὲς τὸ μὴ λήθῃ ὑποπῖπτον{{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 62.51)}}:—**unconcealed**, so **true, real**, opposed to **false, apparent**:
**I** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, opposed to ψευδής,in phrases ἀληθέα μυθήσασθαι, εἰπεῖν, ἀγορεύειν, ἀληθὲς ἐνισπεῖν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.382, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.254, 3.254, 247, +others; in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and _Attic dialect_ τὸ ἀληθές, by Trag. crasis τἀληθές, _Ionic dialect_ τὠληθές (5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.68, 69), or τὰ ἀληθῆ, by crasis τἀληθῆ, etc.}}; ἀληθέϊ λόγῳ χρᾶσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, etc.; οἱ ἀληθέϊ λόγῳ βασιλέες 1.120; ἀληθεστάτη πρόφασις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23.}}
**I.2** of persons, etc., **truthful, honest** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, see below), ἀ. νόος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.92; κατήγορος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 439; κριτής 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.56}}; οἶνος ἀ. `**in vino veritas**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217e; ὁ μέσος ἀ. τις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1108a20.}}
**I.3** of oracles, **true, unerring**, ἀλαθέα μαντίων θῶκον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.6, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 993, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1537}}; of dreams, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 710.}}
**II** of qualities or events, **true, real**, φίλος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 424; ἀ. τὸ πραχθέν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.6}}; **genuine**, ἀ. εἶναι δεῖ τὸ σεμνόν, οὐ κενόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 596.}}
**II.2** **realizing itself, coming to fulfilment**, ἀρά{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 944.}}
**III** _adverb_ ἀληθῶς, _Ionic dialect_ -θέως, **truly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.1, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 315, etc.}}
**III.b** **actually, in reality**, γένος τόδε Ζηνός ἐστιν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 585; ἀ. οὐδὲν ἐξῃκασμένα [prev. author] “Ag.” 1244, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22, etc.}}; τὴν ἀ. μουσικήν (i.e. οὖσαν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 209.6:}} —ὡς ἀ. **in the true way, really**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 739, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63a, etc.; ἡ μὲν γὰρ ὡς ἀ. μήτηρ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.149}}: _comparative_ **-εστέρως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 347e}} **-έστερον** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.4}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.1.}}
**III.2** _neuter_ as _adverb_, _proparoxytone_ ἄληθε; **indeed? really?** ironically, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 350, “Ant.” 758, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 241, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 840, “Av.” 174.}}
**III.3** τὸ ἀληθές **truly**, _Ionic dialect_ τὠληθές{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.70.}}
**B** **not forgetting, careful**, γυνὴ χερνῆτις ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.433, compare 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 24.233}}:—the sense **honest** is post- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} **ἀληθ-ίζω**, **dye with genuine purple,** {{Refs|PHolm. 18.6.}}
**B.II** _middle_ -ίζομαι, ={ἀληθεύω}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136, 3.72, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.230b, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.39, 59.}}

228:
** ἀληθῐνός**, ή, όν,
**agreeable to truth**:
**1** of persons, **truthful, trusty**, στράτευμα, φίλοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.12, compare 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 26}}. _adverb_ -νῶς, φιλεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 9.5}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.37.}}
**2** of things, **true, genuine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 499c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107a31 (_comparative_)}}; especially of purple, πορφυρίς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.3, compare “Edict.Diocl.” 24.6}}; ἰχθύς Amph.26; πέλαγος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 65; λόγος [prev. author] “Sam.” 114}}; τὰ ἀ. **real objects**, opposed to τὰ γεγραμμένα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281b12}}; of persons, ἐς ἀ. ἄνδρ᾽ ἀποβῆναι to turn out a **genuine** man, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.15}}: Astron., **true** (opposed to φαινόμενος **apparent**), of risings and settings, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus 1 “Def.” 1, +others}}
**II** _adverb_ -νῶς **truly, really**, opposed to γλίσχρως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.142}}; ζῶντα ἀ. **really** alive, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 19b; ἀ. γεγάμηκε; 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 221.}}
**II.2** **honestly, straightforwardly**, {{Refs|“OGI” 223.17 (from Erythrae).}}

