231:
** ἁλι-εύς**
(later written ἁλεεύς{{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.19.8, NT.Matt.4.18, “PFlor.” 127.15)}}, ὁ, _genitive_ έως, Ionic ῆος, and _contraction_ ἁλιῶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 200: _accusative_ _plural_ ἁλιέας 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 190.17, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 155.1; _genitive_ ἁλιέων [prev. author] 76.5: (ἅλς, ἅλιος A)}}:—**one who has to do with the sea**, and so,
**1** **fisher**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.251, 22.384, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 115, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 539e, etc.: ἀνὴρ ἁ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 214.}}
**2** **seaman, sailor**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.419}}; ἐρέτας ἁλιῆας rowers **on the sea**, {{Refs|16.349}}; as _adjective_, ἁλιεὺς στρατός {{Refs|Opp.H. 5.121.}}
**3** a fish, ={βάτραχος}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 620b12, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.978d, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.21.5.}}

232:
** ἁλι-εύω**,
(ἅλς) **fish,** {{NT|NT.John.21.3}}; **to be a fisher**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 29}}; **fish for, catch**, σπάρους uncertain reading in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 54; λίθους 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 47: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 65, etc.: _metaphorically_ of avenger, ἁλιεύειν τινάς LXX.Jer.16.16.}}
**II** only _middle_ occurs in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 44}}; Ἁλιευομένη, title of play by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus:—also later, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 68.}}

233:
** ἁλίζω**, [ᾰ],
(ἅλς)
**B** **salt**, _passive_, **to be salted**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 570al, “Pr.” 927a36, LXX.Lev.2.13}}:—_passive_, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|NT.Matt.5.13, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 86.29.}}
**B.II** **supply with salt** or **salt food**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 574a9, +others:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|of sheep, [prev. work] 596a24.}}

235:
** ἀλλά**,
_conjunction_, originally _neuter_ _plural_ of ἄλλος, **otheruise**: used adversatively to limit or oppose words, sentences, or clauses, stronger than δέ:
**I** in simple oppositions, **but**,
**I.1** after _negative_ clauses, οὐ κακός, ἀλλ᾽ ἀγαθός{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 212; οὐδὲ μὲν Ἕκτωρ μίμνεν, ἀλλ᾽.. ἐφορμᾶται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.690, etc.}}
**I.1.b** after a simple _negative_, ἦ παραφρονεῖ; οὔκ, ἀλλ᾽ ὕπνος μ᾽ ἔχει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 9, etc.}}
**I.1.c** frequently after οὐ μόνον, μὴ μόνον, with or without καί, οὐ μόνον ἅπαξ, ἀ. πολλάκις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.13, etc.; without μόνον, οὐχ ἑσπέρας, ἀλλὰ καὶ μεσημβρίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1006: also after οὐχ (or μὴ (ὅτι, οὐχ (or μὴ) ὅπως}}, either, **not only.. but**.., μὴ ὅτι ἰδιώτην τινά, ἀλλὰ τὸν μέγαν βασιλέα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 40d; μὴ ὅτι κατὰ τὸ σῶμα, ἀλλὰ καὶ κατὰ τὴν ψυχήν [prev. author] “Smp.” 207e}}; or, **not only not.. but**.., οὐχ ὅπως κωλυταὶ.. γενήσεσθε, ἀλλὰ καὶ.. περιόψεσθε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.35; οὐχ ὅτι ὠργίζοντο, ἀλλ᾽ ἐζήλουν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.265}}; the _negative_ form is ἀλλ᾽ οὐδέ, μὴ ὅτι ὑπὲρ ἄλλου, ἀλλ᾽ οὐδὲ ὑπὲρ ἐμαυτοῦ δίκην εἴρηκα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.1, etc.}}
**I.2** in the apodosis of hypothetical sentences, **still, at least**, εἴπερ γάρ τε.. ἀλλά τε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.82, etc.: in Prose, especially ἀλλ᾽ οὖν.. γε or ἀλλά.. γε, εἰ καὶ σμικρά, ἀ. οὖν ἴση γε ἡ χάρις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.140}}; εἰ μή (i.e. ὁρῶ), ἀλλ᾽ ἀκούω γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 470d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.15, +others; εἰ μηδέν ἐστι τελευτήσαντι, ἀλλ᾽ οὖν τοῦτόν γε τὸν χρόνον ἧττον ἀηδὴς ἔσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91b (in later Gk. ἀλλά γε}} may be in juxtaposition, εἰ ἄλλοις οὐκ εἰμὶ ἀπόστολος, ἀλλά γε ὑμῖν εἰμί{{NT+8th c.BC+|NT.1Cor.9.2, and ἀ. γε δή is found with vv. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 262a)}}; εἰ καὶ μετέχουσι.. ἀλλ᾽ οὐ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282a11:—less frequently after Conjunctions of Time, as ἐπεὶ δή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.151; ἐπεί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 241.}}
**I.2.b** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἀ. is used elliptically, especially with _adverbs_ of Time, ὦ θεοὶ πατρῷοι, συγγένεσθέ γ᾽ ἀ. νῦν (i.e. εἰ μὴ πρότερον, ἀ. νῦν γε) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 411, compare “Ant.” 552, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 565; ἀ. τῷ χρόνῳ [prev. author] “Med.” 912}}; ἐὰν οὖν ἀ. νῦν γ᾽ ἔτι, i.e.ἐὰν οὖν [μὴἄλλοτε], ἀ. νῦν γε.. if then now **at least** ye still.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.33, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.15}}:—without an _adverb_ of Time, **at least**, ἡ δ᾽ ἀ. πρός σε μικρὸν εἰπάτω μόνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 660, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1276, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 331.}}
**I.3** sometimes = ἀλλ᾽ ἤ (which see), **except, but**, οὔτι μοι αἴτιος ἄλλος, ἀ... τοκῆε no one else, **but**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.312; οὐδέ τις ἄλλη φαίνετο γαιάων, ἀ. οὐρανὸς ἠδὲ θάλασσα 12.404; ἔπαισεν οὔτις ἀ. ἐγώ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1331; ἡδέα.. οὐκ ἔστιν ἀ. τούτοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1176a22, compare 1152b30}}: compare reverse process in our word **but=be out, except**:—sometimes with force of ἤ after comparatives, τάφον, οὐκ ἐν ᾧ κεῖνται μᾶλλον, ἀ.ἐν ᾧ ἡ δόξα κτλ. not that in which they are lying, **but** far more.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43; οὐχ ὅπλων τὸ πλέον, ἀ. δαπάνης [prev. author] 1.83.}}
**I.4** with _negative_ after an affirmative word or clause, to be rendered simply by **not**, ἀγαθῶν, ἀ. οὐχὶ κακῶν αἴτιον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.16; τῶν σπουδαίων, ἀ. μὴ τῶν φαύλων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.2; ἐκεῖθεν, ἀ. οὐκ ἐνθένδε ἡρπάσθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229d:—after a question, τί δεῖ ἐμβαλεῖν λόγον περὶ τούτου, ἀ. οὐχὶ προειπεῖ; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.229, etc.}}
**I.4.b** without _negative_, μικρὸς μὲν ἔην δέμας, ἀ. μαχητής{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.801.}}
**II** to oppose whole sentences,**but, yet**:
**II.1** frequently in transitions, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.135, 140, etc.; ἀ. καὶ ὥς.. 1.116}}; ἀ. οὐδ᾽ ὥς.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.6:—after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in answers and objections, **nay but.., well but**.., frequently with _negatives_, especially in making and answering objections, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 402, 407}}; also in affirmative answers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 330b, “Grg.” 449a, etc.}}:—repeated in a succession of questions or objections, πότερον ᾔτουν σέ τι..; ἀ. ἀπῄτου; ἀ. περὶ παιδικῶν μαχόμενο; ἀ. μεθύων ἐπαρῴνησ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 123e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.47}}; ἀ. μήν.., answered by ἀ.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a23:—in vehement answers 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus often uses νὴ τοὺς θεοὺς ἀ..., μὰ Δἴ ἀ.., “Grg.” 481c, “Phlb.” 36a, cf. Alcaeus1.110b, c:—at beginning of speech, to introduce a general objection, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.472, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 22.}}
**II.2** with _imperative_ or _subjunctive_, to remonstrate, encourage, persuade, etc., frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἀ. ἄγε, ἴθι, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.210, 11.611; ἀ. ἴομεν 6.526; ἀ. πίθεσθε 1.259; after _vocative_, ὦ Φίντις, ἀ. ζεῦξον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.22, compare 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 10.15, etc.}}; answered by a second ἀ., ἀ. περιμένετε. ἀ. περιμενοῦμεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327b; ἀλλ᾽ ἕρπεθ᾽ ὡς τάχιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1643, compare “Ant.” 1029, etc.}}
**II.3** to break off a subject abruptly, ἀ. τά γε Ζεὺς οἶδεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.523}}; ἀ. ταῦτα μὲν τί δεῖλέγει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 11, compare “Tr.” 467, etc.}}
**II.4** in resuming an address after parenthesis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.12, 4.7.}}
**II.5** in elliptical phrases, οὐ μὴν ἀ., οὐ μέντοι ἀ... **it is not [so], but**.., ὁ ἵππος πίπτει καὶ μικροῦ αὐτὸν ἐξετραχήλισεν· οὐ μὴν [ἐξετραχήλισεν] ἀ. ἐπέμεινεν ὁ Κῦρος it did not however [throw him], **but**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.8; οὐ μέντοι ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173b; οὐ γὰρ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 58, 498:—after δέ, ὑμεῖς δέ μ᾽ ἀ. παιδὶ συμφονεύσατε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 391.}}
**III** when joined with other Particles, each retains proper force, as,
**III.1** ἀλλ᾽ ἄρα, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in transitions, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.418, 12.320, etc.}}; later, to introduce an objection, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25a}}; in questions,ἀλλ᾽ ἆρα..; {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 381b.}}
**III.2** ἀλλ᾽ οὖν, concessive, **at all events**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1298; τοὺς πρώτους χρόνους ἀ. οὖνπροσεποιοῦνθ᾽ ὑμῖν εἶναι φίλοι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.86}}; **well then**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310a}}; **but then, however**, with γε following, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.140, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 84, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 652, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.18, etc.; ἀλλ᾽ οὖν γε in apodosi, see above 1.2.}}
**III.3** ἀλλὰ γάρ, frequently with words between, **but really, certainly**, as ἀλλὰ γὰρ Κρέοντα λεύσσω.., παύσω γόους, but this is irregular for ἀλλά, Κρέοντα γὰρ λεύσσω, παύσω γόους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1308, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 148}}; for the regular order{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 81, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 480, “Med.” 1067; frequently elliptical, the Verb being understood, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.8, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 941, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 155: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only with _negatives_, ἀλλ᾽ οὐ γάρ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.242, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.355, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1409}}; ἀ. γὰρ δή, ἀ. γάρ τοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 167, “Ph.” 81.}}
**III.4** ἀ. εἰ.. **quid si.. ? ** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.559.}}
**III.5** ἀ. ἦ in questions, chiefly of surprise or remonstrance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 220, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 879, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1111}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἦ, τὸ λεγόμενον, κατόπιν ἑορτῆς ἥκομε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447a, compare “Prt.” 309c.}}
**III.6** ἀ. followed by strengthening Particle, ἀλλ᾽ ἤτοι μὲν ταῦτα θεῶν ἐν γούνασι κεῖται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.514; especially with _imperative_, 1.211, +others}}; ἀλλά τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.518, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 795, etc.}}; ἀ. μέντοι, with or without γε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 214e, “Hp.Ma.” 287d, +others}}; ἀ. μήν, see at {μή; ἀ. δή}, mostly with words between, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1271, “OC” 586, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.109, etc.}}; without intervening words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37c, +others; ἀ. δῆτα [prev. author] “Hp.Ma.” 285c; ἀ. μὲν δὴ καὶ αὐτός [prev. author] “Tht.” 143b, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 103.}}
**IV** = **et quidem**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 1.13, +others}}

236:
** ἀλλάσσω**,
later _Attic dialect_ **ἀλλάττω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 139a}}: _imperfect_ ἤλλαττον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 466}}: _future_ **-άξω**{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 21}}: _aorist_ ἤλλαξα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 661:}}_perfect_ ἤλλᾰχα (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.6}}, (δι-) {{Refs|Dionysius Epigrammaticus Comedy texts 2.10}}:—_middle_, _future_ ἀλλάξομαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tyr.” 7}}, (ἀντ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1088}}: _aorist_ ἠλλαξάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 103, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.79, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.82}}, etc.: _perfect_ (in _middle_ sense) ἤλλαγμαι (ἐν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 208}}:—_passive_, _future_ ἀλλαχθήσομαι Trag. and Comedy texts, (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 878, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 940}}; ἀλλαγήσομαι in early Prose, (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.120}}, (ἐξαπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28:}}_aorist_ ἠλλάχθην and ἠλλάγην, former more frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus and 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}}, latter in Prose: _perfect_ ἤλλαγμαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 176, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.67, +others}}: _pluperfect_ ἤλλακτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26. (More common in compounds, especially in later Gk.)}}: (ἄλλος):—**make other than it is, change, alter**, τόπον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.41; μορφήν 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 137; χροιάν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1168; ἤλλαττε χρώματ᾽ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 466; τὸ ἑαυτοῦ εἶδος εἰς πολλὰς μορφάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 380d; χώραν [prev. author] “Prm.” 139a.}}
**II** ἀ. τί τινος **give in exchange, barter** one thing **for** another, τῆς σῆς λατρείας τὴν ἐμὴν δυσπραξίαν.. οὐκ ἂν ἀλλάξαιμ᾽ ἐγώ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 967; τι ἀντί τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 661}}:— _middle_, τὴν παραντίκα ἐλπίδα.. οὐδενὸς ἂν ἠλλάξαντο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.82.}}
**II.2** **repay, requite**, φόνον φονεῦσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 89.}}
**II.3** **leave, quit**, οὐράνιον φῶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 944, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 193.}}
**II.4** _middle_, ἔξω τρίβου ἀλλάσσεσθαι ἴχνος **move one's position**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 103.}}
**III** **take** one thing **in exchange for** another, κάκιον τοὐσθλοῦ παρεόντος{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 21}}; πόνῳ πόνον ἀ. **to exchange** one suffering **with** another (nisi to be read πόνου), {{Refs|“Tragica Adespota” 7.3}}; ἠλλαττόμεσθ᾽ ἂν δάκρυα δόντες χρυσίον **should take in exchange**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 73: ἀ. θνητὸν εἶδος}} **assume** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 53, compare 1331:}} —more frequently in _middle_, τί τινος one thing **for** another, εὐδαιμονίας κακοδαιμονίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.79, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 733b}}; τὰ οἰκήϊα κακὰ ἀλλάξασθαι τοῖσι πλησίοισι **exchange** them with them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152}}: hence, **buy**, τι ἀντ᾽ ἀργυρίου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371c; διά τινος ὠνῆς ἢ καὶ πράσεως ἀλλάττεσθαί τί τινι [prev. author] “Lg.” 915d, 915e}}; τοῦ παντὸς ἀ. **prize above** all things, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 56.30.}}
**III.2** **take a new position**, i.e. **go to** a place, ἀ.Ἅιδα θαλάμους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 483; πόλιν ἐκ πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 289e.}}
**IV** _absolutely_, **have dealings**, as buyer or seller, in _middle_, πρός τινα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 915e.}}
**IV.2** **alternate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.6}}; σκῆπτρ᾽ ἔειν ἐνιαυτὸν ἀλλάσσοντε to enjoy power **in turn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 74, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 42c:}} —_passive_, ἀρεταὶ.. ἀλλασσόμεναι **in turns**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.38, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 940a15}}. ϝ. _passive_, **to be reconciled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 997.}}

238:
** ἀλληγορ-έω**,
(ἀγορεύω) **interpret allegorically**, Ἕλληνες Κρόνον ἀλληγοροῦσι τὸν χρόνον{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.363d, compare 996b, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus “Allegoriae Quaestiones Homericae” 1}}:—_passive_, **to be spoken allegorically,** {{NT|NT.Gal.4.24}}; ἀλληγορεῖται ἁ Ἀπόλλων εἰς τὸν Ἥλιον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 186.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **speak figuratively** or **metaphorically**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 151, 285}}; **speak allegorically**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “AJProoem.”}}

242:
** ἅλλομαι**,
{{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 175, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἡλλόμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.11, etc.:}}_future_ ἁλοῦμαι (ὑπερ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.4}}, _Doric dialect_ ἁλεῦμ αι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.25, 5.144}}: _aorist 1_ ἡλάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 225, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1402, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 243, no}}_subjunctive_ or _optative_, _participle_ ἁλάμενος [_1st pers._ syllable long] {{Refs|“Av.” 1395}}, _infinitive_ ἅλασθαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 16}}, (καθ-) variant {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 14.5:}}_aorist 2_ ἡλόμην, rare in _indicative_, variant {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “HG” 4.4.11}}, (ἐξ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1311}}, (ἐν-) variant {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 516}}_subjunctive_ ἅληται [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.536, _optative_ ἁλοίμην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.9 (compare εἰσ-)}}, _infinitive_ ἁλέσθαι{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.83}}, etc., _participle_ ἁλόμενος [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 368 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.17}}, etc.; to _aorist 2_ also belong _Epic dialect_ {{Refs|2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἆλσο, ἆλτο}}, _subjunctive_ ἅλεται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.192}}_participle_ ἄλμενος only in compounds, but ἅλμενος{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 5.666}}: (**sal-**, cf. Latin _sal-io_):— **spring, leap**, properly of living beings, μὴ.. ἐς τεῖχος ἅληται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.536; ἐπεί κ᾽.. εἰς ἵππους ἅλεται 11.192; εἰς ἅλα ἆλτο 1.532 (but ἥλατο πόντον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 195); ἐξ ὀχέων.. ἆλτο χαμᾶζε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.103; ἆλτο κατ᾽ Οὐλύμπου 18.616:}} —ἅλλεσθαι ἐπί τινι **leap upon** or **against**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.174, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.80; ἐπὶ στίχας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.353: with}}_infinitive_, ἆλτο θέειν, πέτεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 389, “Ap.” 448: _absolutely_, of horse, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.4.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **leap over**, βόθρον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 6.6; τάφρον 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.83.}}
**3** of things, ἆλτο ὀϊστός{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.125}}; of sound, ἀπὸ λείων ἠχὼ ἁλλομένη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Phdr.” 255c}}; of parts of body, **twitch, quiver, throb**, ἅλλεται ὀφθαλμός{{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.37, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 604a27, “PRyl.” 1.28.}}

243:
** ἄλλος**, η, ο,
{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria αἶλος “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135 H. (from Idalion): (from ἀλ}} **y**ος, cf. Latin _alius_):—**another**, i. e. **one besides** what has been mentioned, either _adjective_ or _pronoun_: when _adjective_, its _substantive_ is either in the same case, or in _genitive_, Ζεῦ ἄλλοι τε θεοί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.476; θεῶν ἄ. 16.446:}} —ἄ. μέν.. ἄ. δέ.. **one.. another**.., more rarely **the one.. the other.. ** (of **two** persons, etc.), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.493, etc.}}; τὰ μέν.. ἄλλα δέ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.147}}, and _Attic dialect_; ἕτερον μέν.. ἄλλον δέ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.313}}; ἄλλο μέν.. ἑτέρου δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; θάτερον.. τὸ δ᾽ ἄλλο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 962.}}
**II** with τις, **any other**, οὐδέ τις ἄ. ἔγνω ἀλλ᾽ ἄρα Κασσάνδρη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.697; ἄ. τις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.85}}; οὐδεὶς ἄ. no **other**, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; ἄλλα πολλά[{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.639; πολλὰ καὶ ἄλλα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.56; for εἴ τις ἄλλος [prev. author] 6.32, etc., and εἴ τις καὶ ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.15, etc., see at {εἰ}.}}]
**II.2** frequently with another of its own cases or derived Adverbs, ἄ. ἄλλα λέγει **one man** says **one thing, one another**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.15; ἄ. ἄλλω᾽ ἔλεγεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c; ἄ. ἄλλῃ ἐτράπετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.19}}; see at {ἄλλοθεν, ἄλλοσε, ἄλλοτε}; also with Verb in _plural_, παραλαμβάνων ἄ. ἄλλον ἐπ᾽ ἄλλου, τὸν δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἄλλου χρείᾳ.. ἐθέμεθα πόλιν ὄνομα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.4, etc.: _plural_, ἄλλοι}} when the several parties are _plural_, λείπουσι τὸν λόφον.. ἄλλοι ἄλλοθεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.13.}}
**II.3** ἄ. καὶ ἄ., **one and then another, one or two**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.12}}; ἄλλο καὶ ἄλλο **one thing after another**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.1.15}}; πρὸς ἄλλὡ καὶ ἄλλὡ σημείὡ to **different** points, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.7.}}
**II.4** repeated for emphasis, ἄ. ἄ. τρόπος **quite another** sort, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 132.}}
**II.5** οὐδ᾽ ἄ. for οὐδέτερος, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.45.}}
**II.6** with _Article_, ὁ ἄλλος, **the rest, all besides**; in _plural_, οἱ ἄλλοι (_Ionic dialect_ _contraction_ ὧλλοι) **all the others, the rest**, frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards (ἄλλοι in same signification, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.1)}}; τὰ ἄλλα, _contraction_ τἆλλα, **all else**, τἆλλα πλὴν ὁ χρυσός{{Refs|“Scol.” 1 (Pytherm.)}}; in _Attic dialect_ frequently as _adverb_, **for the rest**, especially in amendments to decrees, τὰ μὲν ἄλλα καθάπερ ὁ δεῖνα κτλ. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 1.27a70, etc.: of Time, ={τὸν ἄλλον χρόνον}, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.2}}]; ὁ ἄ. χρόνος, ={ὁ λοιπὸς χρόνος}, of the future, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.4}} (but also of the past, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.16)}}; τῇ ἄλλῃ ἡμέρᾳ, τῷ ἄλλῳ ἔτει, **next** day, **next** year, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.13, 1.2.1}}; οἵτε ἄλλοι καί.. **all others** and especially.., γυναῖκας ἄλλας τε πολλὰς καὶ δὴκαὶ βασιλέος θυγατέρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, etc.}}; ἄλλα τε δὴ εἶπε, καί.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142c; (see. ἄλλως 1):}} —τὸ ἄλλο is much less frequently than τὰ ἄλλα.
**II.7** with Numerals, **yet, still, further**, τρίτον ἄ. γένος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 143}}; πέμπτος ποταμὸς ἄ. **yet** a fifth river, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.54, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 486, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1295, etc.}}
**II.8** in enumerations, **as well, besides**, ἅμα τῇγε καὶ ἀμφίπολοι κίον ἄλλαι with her their mistress came attendants **also**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.84; μήτηρ ἠδὲ πατὴρ ἠδ᾽ ἄλλοι πάντες ἑταῖροι 9.367}}; οὐ γὰρ ἦν χόρτος οὐδὲ ἄ. δένδρον οὐδέν there was no grass nor any tree **at all**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.5; πολιτῶν καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ξένων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473d}}; προσοφλὼν οὐ τὴν ἐπωβελίαν μόνον ἀλλὰ καὶ ἄλλην ὕβριν **besides**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.163:—pleonastic, παρ᾽ ἀγγέλων ἄλλων ἀκούειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2; ἰδὼν ἐς πλησίον ἄλλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.81; γυναικῶν τῶν ἄλλων μία 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 945; μόνη τῶν ἄλλων ἐπιστημῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 166e}}; with _comparative_, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, οὔτις σεῖο νεώτερος ἄ. Ἀχαιῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.569, compare 22.106, +others}}; with _superlative_, ὀϊζυρώτατος ἄλλων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.105.}}
**III** less frequently, ={ἀλλοῖος}, **of other sort, different**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.64, 21.22; ἄ. γέγονεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241a.}}
**III.2** in this sense, with _genitive_, ἄλλα τῶν δικαίων **other than** just, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.25:}} —followed by ἤ.., with preceding _negative_, οὐδὲ ἄλλο.., οὐδὲν ἄλλο (or ἄλλο οὐδέν).., ἤ.. nothing **else** than.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.49, 7.168, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.14; οὐδὲν ἄλλο γ᾽ ἤπτήξας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 209}}; ἃ μηδὲν ἄλλο ἢ διανεῖταί τις which one **only** thinks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 195e:—more frequently in questions, τίς ἄλλος ἢ 'γώ..; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 440}}; τί δ᾽ ἄλλογ᾽ ἢπόνοι..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 852: elliptically, τί ἄλλο (i.e. πάσχω) ἢ ἱπποκένταυρος γίγνομα; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.20}}; τί ἄλλο (i.e. ἐποίησαν) ἢ ἐπεβούλευσα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39:—followed by πλήν, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 125, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 39}}; by Preps., πρό..{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.85}}; ἀντί.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 467}}; παρά.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 80b, etc.: with _negative_, sometimes followed by ἀλλά, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.403, 21.275:}} —see also ἄλλο τι.
**III.3** **other than what is, untrue, unreal**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.348.}}
**III.4** **other than right, wrong, bad**, ἄλλου τινος ἡττῆσθαι yield to some **unworthy** motive, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.218, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.187d, etc.}}; compare ἄλλως.

245:
** ἀλλότριος**,
_Aeolic dialect_ ἀλλότερρος {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 529.24}}, α, ον, (ἄλλος) **of** or **belonging to another**, βίοτος, νηῦς, ἄχεα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.160, 9.535, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.298}}; γυνή **another man's** wife, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 448 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀλλοτρίων χαρίσασθαι to be bountiful **of what is another's,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.452}}; γναθμοῖσι γελοίων ἀλλοτρίοισιν with faces **unlike their own,** of a **forced, unnatural** laugh, {{Refs|[prev. work] 20.347}}; ἀ. ὄμμασιν εἷρπον by the help of **another's** eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 146(Lyric poetry)}}; οὐκ ἀ. ἄτην not **inflicted by other hands,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1259}}; but ἀ. φόνος murder **of a stranger**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(compare 11.1), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4b: proverbial, ἀ. ἀμᾶν θέρος}} reap **where one has not sown,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 392, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 599}}; ἀλλοτριωτάτοις τοῖς σώμασιν χρῆσθαι deal with one's body **as if it belonged to another,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; τὰ ἀλλότρια, _contraction_ τἀλλότρια, **what belongs to others, not one's own,** τἀ. ἀποστερεῖν, δειπνεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 4.1, Theopomp. Comedy texts 34.}}
**II** opposed to οἰκεῖος, **foreign, strange,**
**II.1** of persons, ἀ. φώς **stranger,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.219, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 481}}; almost = **enemy,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.214, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.102}}; οὐδέ τις ἀλλοτρίων no **stranger,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155; εἴτε ἀ. εἴτε οἰκεῖος ὁ τεθνεώς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4b; ἀ. τῆς πόλεως 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.6; οὐδείς ἐστί μοι ἀ., ἂν ᾖ χρηστός 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 602}}; ἀλλοτριώτερος τῶνπαίδων **less near** than thy children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.119}}; ἀλλοτριώτερος, opposed to οἰκειότερος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1162a3: with _dative_, ἀλλότριοι ὑμῖν ὄντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.51.}}
**II.1.b** **hostile, unfavourably disposed,** with _genitive_, ἀ. Ῥωμαίων{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.4.4; -ώτατος μοναρχίας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.65; ἀλλότρια φρονῶν τοῦ βασιλέως 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 36.15.7, compare “OGI” 90.19 (from Rosetta).}}
**II.1.c** **disinclined,** πρὸς τὰς κακοπαθείας{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 36.15.2.}}
**II.2** of things, **alien, strange,** τροφή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 491d (_comparative_), etc.}}; εἴ τι πρότερον γέγονεν ἀ. **estrangement,** Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.185; ἡ ἀ.}}**alien country, enemy's country,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.50, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.73}}: with _genitive_, **alien from,** ἐπιτηδεύματα δημοκρατίας ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.34}}; οὐδὲν ἀ. ποιῶν τοῦ τρόπου Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.182.}}
**II.2.b** in Medicine texts, **abnormal,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.780}}; ἀ. σάρκες **superfluous** fat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556d.}}
**II.2.c** **foreign to the purpose,** λόγοι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1218b23}}: _comparative_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1159b24}}: _superlative_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cat.” 15b29, cf. Polystr. p.17 W.}}
**II.2.d** Astrology texts, ={ἀπόστροφος}, {{Refs|“POxy.” 464.16.}}
**III** _adverb_ ἀλλοτρίως, διακεῖσθαι πρὸς ἀλλήλας to be **unfavourably** disposed towards.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.159}}; ἄ. ἔχειν πρός.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.80}}: _comparative_ **-ιώτερον** **less favourably,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.9.}}
**III.2** **strangely, marvellously,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 989.2.}}
**III.3** **in a manner foreign to,** with _genitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Persaeus Citieus Stoicus 1.1co. [-ο ¯ τρ- only in 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 557 assuming variant]}}

247:
** ἄλλως**,
_Doric dialect_ ἀλλῶς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 175.13}}_adverb_ of ἄλλος, **otherwise,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.401, etc.: frequently with other _adverbs_, ἄ. πως}} **in** some **other way,** ἄ. οὐδαμῶς **in** no **other wise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 343b, 526a, etc.; πως ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.39; ἄ. καὶ ἄ. 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 23p.468M.}}
**2** καὶ ἄλλως and **besides** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (ἄλλος 11.8), ἀγήνωρ ἐστὶ καὶ ἄλλως 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.699}}; a woman is described as very tall καὶ ἄ. εὐειδής{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, etc.; ἀρίστου καὶ ἄ. φρονιμωτάτου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 118:—so ἄ. δέ.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.105, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1476; ἄλλως τε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1114, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.142.}}
**2.b** **at all events, any how,** εἴ πέρ γε καὶ ἄ. ἐθέλει.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16}}.; ἄλλως alone, εἰ ἄ. βούλοιτο{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.30; ἐπείπερ ἄλλως.. εἰς Ἄργος κίεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 680.}}
**3** frequently in phrase ἄλλως τε καί.. **both otherwise and**.., i.e. **especially, above all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 473, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70, etc.}}; strengthened, ἄ. τε πάντως καί.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 636, “Eu.” 726}}; frequently followed by ἤν, εἰ, ἐπειδή, **especially** if.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 21, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, 2.3}}; by _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.104, 7.80:—without καί, ἄ. τε ἐάν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.59; ἄλλως τε ἐπειδή 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85e, etc.}}
**II** **otherwise than** something implied, **differently,** τοῦτ᾽ οὐκ ἔστιν ἄ. εἶπαι to deny it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.124}}; οὐκ ἄ. λέγω I say no **otherwise,** i.e. I say **so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 302: hence}}
**II.2** **far otherwise,** i.e. **better,** οὐδέ κεν ἄλλως οὐδὲ θεὸς τεύξειε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.176, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.53.}}
**II.3** more frequently, **otherwise than should be, at random, without aim or purpose,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.124, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.16, 4.77, etc.}}:—**in uain,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.144; frequently in Trag. and Comedy texts, ἀλλ᾽ ἄ. πονεῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1151, compare 333, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1030, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 11}}; with _substantive_, εἴδωλον ἄ. **mere** image, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 947; ἀριθμὸν ἄ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 476; παρὰ καιρὸν ἄ. [prev. author] “IA” 800; ἀριθμός, πρόβατ᾽ ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1203; ὄχλος ἄ. καὶ βασκανία 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.24, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.78}}; τὴν ἄ., i.e. ἄγουσαν ὁδόν, **in vain,** λέγειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.21; ψηφίζεσθαι 19.181, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 51, etc.}}; also, **in no particular way,** i.e. **concerning indiffcrent matters,** οἱ ἀγῶνες οὐδέποτε τὴν ἄ., ἄλλ᾽ ἀεὶ τὴν περὶ αὑτοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172e; τὴν ἄ. θεωρεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 650a}}; τήν γε ἄλλως **otherwise,** i.e. **generally,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.24, 42.50}}:—**for nothing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.139}}: —**otherwise than right, wrongly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 1.12, etc.}}

248:
** ἀλοάω**,
_Epic dialect_ ἀλοιάω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.48}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀλοία{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.568}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{LXX|LXX.Jer.5.17}}, _Doric dialect_ ἀλοιησέω{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus in “Berl.Sitzb.” 1918.728}}: _aorist_ ἠλόησα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 149, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.34 (ἀλοιήσῃ [prev. work] 51)}}_participle_ ἀλοάσας [ᾱς] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 65}}; _Epic dialect_ ἠλοίησα (ἀπ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.522}}, (συν-) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.128}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ηθήσομαι**{{LXX|LXX.Jer.28(51).33}}: _aorist_ ἠλοήθην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.6.5, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.12.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.327a}}: _perfect_ ἠλόημαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.12.9 (Codex Urb.):}} — _poetry_ _aorist_ _participle_ ἀλοίσας (as if from ἀλοίω,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 775.8, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Epim.” 277) uncertain reading in epigram cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.31}}; ἀλοϝάω uncertain in {{Refs|“Glotta” 4.202 (archaic Apulian vase)}}:—**tread, thresh,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 124a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.2, LXX.Deu.25.4.}}
**2** **thresh, smite,** γῆν χερσὶν ἀλοία{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 0.568, compare epigram [same place]; μηρόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 2}}; **cudgel, thrash, Glotta** [same place], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 20 (uncertain), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 149, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.34; ῥοπάλῳ τινά 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 98.15.}}
**3** **crush, smash,** σκεύη{{Refs|[prev. author] 129.16}}; **destroy,** πόλεις{{LXX|LXX.Jer.5.17.}}
**II** **drive round and round,** like cattle treading out corn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 2 (_accusative_ to Scholia).}}

249:
** ἄλογος**, ον,
**without** λόγος, hence,
**I** **speechless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 696e}}. _adverb_ **-ως** **without speech,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 131, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.9:}} —ἄ. ἡμέρα, = Latin _dies nefastus_, **on which no business may be done,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Legal.” 9.}}
**I.b** **lacking in eloquence,** {{LXX|LXX.Exo.6.12.}}
**I.2** **inexpressive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 203a}}; **unutterable,** ={ἄρρητος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 262.}}
**II** **unreasoning,** ἡδονή, ὄχλος, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 591c, “Ti.” 42d, etc.; τὰ ἄλογα}}**brutes, animals,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 164, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 7.3}}; especiallyin late Greek, ἄλογον, τό, = **horse,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 138.29 (610 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus), “PGen.” 14 (late).}}
**II.2** **not according to reason, irrational,** ἄ. δόξα, opposed to ἡ μετὰ λόγου δ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 201c}}; ἀλόγῳ πάθει τὴν ἄ. συνασκεῖν αἴσθησιν, **instinctive** feeling, in appreciating works of art, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 11; ἄ. πάθος [prev. author] “Comp.” 23.}}
**II.3** **contrary to reason, absurd,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 203d}}; **unaccountable, unintelligible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.19}}; **unfit, unsuited to its end,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32}}; **groundless,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.15.9; ἀηδία “PRyl.” 144.15 (38 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus)}}. _adverb_ most frequently in this sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439d, etc.; οὐκ ἀ. οὐδ᾽ ἀκαίρως 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.10}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.44 W.}}
**III** **without reckoning:**
**III.1** **not reckoned upon, unexpected,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46 (_comparative_).}}
**III.2** **not counted, null and void,** ἡμέραι{{LXX|LXX.Num.6.12.}}
**III.3** _active_, **not having paid one's reckoning,** of an ἐρανιστής, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 70.31.}}
**IV** of magnitudes, **incommensurable,** περὶ ἀλόγων γραμμῶν, title of work by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76b9, “LI” 968b18, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 10. “Def.” 10, etc.}}
**IV.2** in Rhythm, **irrational,** of feet or syllables whose time-relations cannot be expressed by a simple ratio, χορεῖος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Rhythmica” 2.20}}; ἄλογοι, i.e. συλλαβαί, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 20:—in Music, ἄ. διαστήματα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1145d}}:—of the pulse, **unrhythmical,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “synopsis de pulsibus” 4.3.}}

250:
** ἀλόη**, ἡ,
**bitter aloes, Aloe vera,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.22, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.141f, etc.}}
**2** ={ἀγάλλοχον}, {{LXX|LXX.Cant.4.15}} (in Heb. form ἀλώθ), {{NT|NT.John.19.39.}}
**3** ἀ. γαλλική, ={γεντιανή}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.3.}}
**4** ἀ. ἡπατῖτις, **hepatic aloes, Aloe Perryi,** {{Refs|Gp. 6.6.2.}}

253:
** ἄλῡπ-ος**, ον,
**without pain,** frequently in Trag. (not {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 163, etc.: with _genitive_, ἄ. γήρως}} **without pains** of age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1519; ἄ. ἄτης “El.” 1002; βίος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1004; ἀρχή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 593; τὸ ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 585a: _comparative_ -ότερος [prev. work] 581e}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος**{{Refs|“Lg.” 848e.}}_adverb_ ἀλύπως, ζῆν, διατελεῖν live **free from pain and sorrow,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 358b, “Phlb.” 43d, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 549; ἀποθανεῖν [prev. author] 14}}: _superlative_ ἀλυπότατα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.10.}}
**II** _active_, **causing no pain** or **grief,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 39 (_superlative_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 272a, etc.; ἄ. οἶνος}}**harmless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 82.5, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 423}}; ἄ. ἄνθος ἀνίας **setting free from the pain** of sorrow of wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 172}}; ἀλυπότατος κλιντήρ, of a hospice, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 450 (from Batanaea)}}; σωλῆνες -ότατοι μετὰ ἁλῶν **cause least pain,** i.e. **are least indigestible,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Xenocrates Medicus 57, cf. Mnesith. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.92c; πεσσὸς -ότατος 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.36.}}_adverb_ ἀλύπως, τοῖς ἄλλοις ζῆν live **without offence** to others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.5.}}
**III** ἄλυπον, τό, **herb terrible, Globularia Alypum,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 27.22, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.178.}}

254:
** ἅλῠσις**
(on the breathing see Hdn.Gr. 1.539), εως, ἡ, **chain,** χαλκέῃ ἁλύσι δεδεμένη ἄγκυρα{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.74, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76, etc.; ἐν ἁλύσει μιᾷ δεδεμένους 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 30.17, cf. NT.Eph.6.20; πέτραν ἁλύσεσι χρυσέαισι φερομέναν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 982:—as a woman's ornament, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 320.12, 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus 33; σφραγῖδε.. ἁλύσεις χρυσᾶς ἔχουσαι “IG” 2.652 B 35.}}
**2** collectively, **chains, bondage,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.3.3.}}
**3** **link** in chain armour, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 3.5.}}

255:
** ἀλῡσῐ-τελής**, ές,
**unprofitable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 417d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 14.5, Polystr. p.18}} W.; of a person, ἀ. τῇ πόλει{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 2.9}}: _superlative_ **-έστατος**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.105}}. _adverb_ **-λῶς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.7.2, 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 12p.447M., etc.}}
**II** in Medicine texts, **unfavourable,** of symptoms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 14.}}

258:
** ἀλώπηξ** [ᾰ], εκος
(also ἀλώπηκος{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Ananius Lyricus 5)}}, ; _dative_ _plural_, ἀλώπεξι{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.21.10}}, _Epic dialect_ ἀλωπήκεσσι{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.433}}:—**fox, Canis vulpes** (smaller Egyptian species {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 606a24}}, **C. niloticus**), {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 86.2, 89.5, 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.7, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.67, etc.}}: of sly persons, ἀλώπεκος ἴχνεσι βαίνειν{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5}}; μῆτιν ἀ. a **very fox** for craft, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).65: proverbial, τὴν.. Ἀρχιλόχου ἀλώπεκα ἑλκτέον ἐξόπισθεν}} we must trail Archilochus' **foxskin** behind, i.e. deceive by false appearances, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365c; πολλῆς αὐτῆς τῆς ἀ. ἐπιχέαντες 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.249D.}}; ἡ ἀ. τὸν βοῦν ἐλαύνει 'sleight masters might', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 2.73}}; πεινῶσαν ἀ. ὕπνος ἐπέρχεται 'qui dort dine', {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.91}}; ἡ κέρκος τῇ ἀλώπεκι μαρτυρεῖ 'ex pede Herculem', {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.15}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ αὖθις ἀ. (i.e. πάγαις ἁλώσεται) 'a burnt child dreads the fire', {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.15.}}
**II** a large **bat, Sciurus** or **Pteromys volans,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 490a7.}}
**III** ={ἀλωπεκίας} {{Refs|11, [prev. work] 566a31.}}
**IV** in _plural_, **muscles of the loins, psoas-muscles,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 92, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 189.}}
**V** ={ἀλωπεκία} {{Refs|1}}**mange,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.72, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 79}}: in _plural_, **bald patches,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ παθῶν” 35.}}
**VI** kind of **dance,** uncertain in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 419 (probably in sense see), compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {ὄρχησις}.}}]

259:
** ἅλ-ωσις**, εως,
_Ionic dialect_ ιος, ἡ, **capture,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).42, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5, 3.156, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 589, etc.}}; δαΐων ἅ. **conquest** by enemy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 119}}: **means of conquest,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 61.}}
**2** **taking, catching** of birds and fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 593a20, 600a3 (_plural_)}}; ἑαλωκότες ἰσχυρὰν ἅλωσιν taken **without power to escape,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 15.}}
**II** law-term, **conviction,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 920a, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 97.3.}}

260:
** ἅμᾰ** [ᾰμ],
_Doric dialect_ ἁμᾶ, which see: (see. entry near the end):
**A** _adverb_ **at once, at the same time,** mostly of Time, frequently added to τε.. καί, ἅμ᾽ οἰμωγή τε καὶ εὐχωλή{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.64; ἅ. τ᾽ ὠκύμορος καὶ ὀϊζυρός 1.417; σέ θ᾽ ἅ. κλαίω καὶ ἐμέ 24.773; σαυτόν θ᾽ ἅ. κἀμέ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 772, compare 119; ἄνους τε καὶ γέρων ἅ. “Ant.” 281:—with καί only, ἅ. πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.109; with τε.. τε, χειρῶν τε βίης θ᾽ ἅ. ἔργον ἔφαινον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 677.}}
**A.2** ἅ. μέν.. ἅ. δέ.., **partly.. partly..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115d, X “HG” 3.1.3:— ἅ. τε.. καὶ ἅ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497a; ἅμ᾽ ἡδέως ἔμοιγε κἀλγεινῶς ἅμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 436.}}
**A.3** in Prose ἅ. δέ.. καί.., ἅ. τε.. καί.., ἅ... καί.. may often be translated by **no sooner.. than..,** ἅ. δὲ ταῦτα ἔλεγε καὶ ἀπεδείκνυε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112; ταῦγά τε ἅμα ἠγόρευε καὶ πέμπει 8.5; ἅ. ἀκηκόαμέν τε καὶ τριηράρχους καθίσταμεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.36; ἅ. διαλλάττονται καὶ τῆς ἔχθρας ἐπιλανθάνονται 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.157.}}
**A.3.b** ἅ. μῦθος ἔην, τετέλεστο δὲ ἔργον 'no sooner said than done', {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.242; ἅ. ἔπος τε καὶ ἔργον ἐμήδετο “hymnus ad Mercurium” 46; ταῦτα εἶπε καὶ ἅ. ἔπος τε καὶ ἔργον ἐποίεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, compare 9.92: proverbial, ἅμ᾽ ἔπος ἅμ᾽ ἔργον 4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 1.36.}}
**A.3.c** with _participle_ and finite Verb in same sense, βρίζων ἅ... ἐξήμελξας εὐτραφὲς γάλα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 897}}; ἅ. εἰπὼν ἀνέστη **as soon as** he had done speaking, he stood up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.47}}; τῆς ἀγγελίας ἅ. ῥηθείσης ἐπεβοήθουν **as soon as** news was brought they assisted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5; ἅ. γιγνόμενοι λαμβάνομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 76c; ἡμῖν ἅ. ἀναπαυομένοις ὁ παῖς ἀναγνώσεται “Tht.” 143b.}}
**A.4** ἅ. μέν.. ἔτι δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.3; ἅ. μέν.. πρὸς δέ.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.51.}}
**A.II** **together, at once, both,** without direct _reference_ to time, ἅ. πάντες or πάντες ἅ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.495, +others; ἅ. ἄμφω “hymnus ad Cererem” 15; ἅ. κρατερὸς καὶ ἀμύμων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.111, etc.: of Place, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1028b27.}}
**A.III** with σύν or μετά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 717, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 110a.}}
**A.IV** _absolutely_ with Verb, **at one and the same time,** αἱ πᾶσαι [νῆες] ἅ. ἐγίγνοντο ἐν ἑνὶ θέρει σ καὶ ν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.17, compare οὐχ ἅ. ἡ κτῆσις παραγίγνεται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.113.}}
**B** _preposition_ with _dative_ (frequently with _participle_ added), **at the same time with, together with,** ἅμ᾽ ἠοῖ φαινομένηφι **at** dawn, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.682, +others}}; ἅ. ἕῳ, ἅ. ἕῳ γιγνομένῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.48, 4.32}}; ἅμ᾽ ἠελίῳ ἀνιόντι or καταδύντι **at** sunrise or sunset, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.136, 210, +others; ἅμ᾽ ἡμέρῃ διαφωσκούσῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.86, +others; ἅμ᾽ ἡμέρᾳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 78, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.94, etc.}}, _Attic dialect_; ἅμ᾽ ἦρι ἀρχομένῳ or ἅ. ἦρι **at** beginning of spring, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.20, 2.2, etc.; ἅ. κήδεϊ κεκάρθαι τὰς κεφαλάς}}**during the time of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36; ἅ. τειχισμῷ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.20; ἅμα τῷ διαυγάζειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.104.5 (without _Article_ ἅμα εὑρεθῆναι Pseudo-Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 23.2).}}
**B.2** generally, **together with,** ἅ. τινὶ στείχειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.257; ὀπάσσαι 24.461, +others; Ἑλένην καὶ κτήμαθ᾽ ἅμ᾽ αὐτῇ 3.458}}; ἅ. πνοιῇς ἀνέμοιο **keeping pace with** the wind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.98; repeated, ἅμ᾽ αὐτῷ.. ἅμ᾽ ἕποντο 11.371; οἱ ἅ. Θόαντι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.138, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.57.}}
**B.II** rarely with _genitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.95, “POxy.” 903 (4th c.AD), 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus “Sim.” 28, 5th c.AD: Olympiodorus Historicus p.453 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; uncertain in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 6.9.}}
**C** _conjunction_, **as soon as,** ἅ. ἂν ἡβήσῃ τις τῶν ὀρφανῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 928c, cf. Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.20; ἅ. κα διεξέλθῃ ὁ χρόνος “GDI” 2160 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}. (Root **sṃ-,** cf. A α 11.)

261:
** ἀμαθ-ής**, ές,
(μαθεῖν) **ignorant, stupid,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.33, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 169, etc.; ἔθνεα ἀμαθέστατα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.46; ἀνὴρ πένης, εἰ καὶ γένοιτο μὴ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 421, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 135; ἀ. καὶ βδελυρός [prev. author] “Eq.” 193; ἀμαθεστάτους πάντων ἀνθρώπων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.2; ἀ. τὴν [ἐκείνων] ἀμαθίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22e}}; opposed to δεξιός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; of animals, such as wild boars, **unmanageable,** θυμώδη καὶ ἀ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488b14}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **without knowledge of** a thing, **unlearned in** it, -έστερος τοῦ καλοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 417; λῃστείας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.41, compare 3.37; ἀ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 394e}}; τι {{Refs|“La.” 194d; πρός τι “Lg.” 679d.}}_adverb_ -θῶς **ignorantly, through ignorance,** ἥμαρτον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 874: _comparative_ -έστερον, τῶν νόμων ὑπεροψίας παιδεύεσθαι}} to be educated **with too little learning** to despise the laws, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84; -έστερόν πως εἰπὲ καὶ σαφέστερον}}**less learnedly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1445.}}
**b** of moral defects, **unfeeling, inhuman,** ἀ. τις εἶ θεός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 347.}}
**2** of things, ἀ. παρρησία **boorish** freedom of speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 905}}; ἀ. ῥώμη **brute** force, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “fragment” 732; ἀ. δύναμις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 42}}; ἀ. φρόνημα **barbarous** pride, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 459.}}
**II** **not heard of, unknown,** ἀ. ἔρρει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 916.}}_adverb_ ἀμαθῶς, χωρῆσαι, of events, to take **an unforeseen course,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140.}}

262:
** ἀμᾰράντινος**, η, ον,
**of amaranth,** i. e. **unfading,** στέφανος{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 19.14.}}
**2** **unfading, imperishable,** στέφανος{{NT|NT.1Pet.5.4.}}

263:
** ἀμάραντος** [ᾰμᾰ], ον,
(μαραίνω) **unfading,** λειμών{{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 9: _metaphorically_, σοφία LXX.Wis.6.12; κληρονομία NT.1Pet.1.4, compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2942c (from Tralles)}}; πνεῦμα probably in {{Refs|“IPE” 2.286 (from Panticapaeum)}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.9.}}
**II** _substantive_ ἀμάραντον, τό (but in Latin _amarantus_), **never-fading flower,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.607e (from Carales), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.229}}; ={ἑλίχρυσον}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.57}}; ={κενταύρειον μικρόν}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.7}}; ={χρυσοκόμη}. {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.55.}}

264:
** ἁμαρτάνω** [ᾰμ.. ᾰν]:
_future_ ἁμαρτήσομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.512, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, etc.}} later **-ήσω** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.18.21, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.20, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.653}}, (δι-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 9}}, (ἐξ-) {{Refs|[prev. author]}}**Acut. (Sp.)**{{Refs|13:}}_aorist 2_ ἥμαρτον{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}}, _Attic dialect_; _Epic dialect_ ἤμβροτον, but only _indicative_; _Aeolic dialect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ ἄμβροτε{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 1.5}}, _infinitive_ ἀμβρότην{{Refs|“IG” 12(2).1.15 (from Mytilene)}}; _optative_ ἁμάρτοιν (for ἁμάρτοιμι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 55 (uncertain)}}: _aorist 1_ ἡμάρτησα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 115.4 (uncertain), “Anthologia Graeca” 7.339 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus or 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.14}}: _perfect_ ἡμάρτηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.79, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 961, etc.}}, _Attic dialect_:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἡμαρτήθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.37}}: _perfect_ ἡμάρτημαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 439, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.77, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἡμάρτητο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.18, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.20}}:—**miss the mark,** especially of spear thrown, _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.287, etc.; ἔρριψεν, οὐδ᾽ ἥμαρτε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 80: with _genitive_, φωτὸς ἁ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.372; also τῶν μεγάλων ψυχῶν ἱεὶς οὐκ ἂν ἁμάρτοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 155}}; ἁ. τῆς ὁδοῦ **miss** road, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 961; τοῦ σκοποῦ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.5.}}
**2** generally, **fail of one's purpose, go wrong,** _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1194, etc.: with _genitive_, οὔ τι νοήματος ἤμβροτεν ἐσθλοῦ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.292}}; μύθων ἡμάρτανε **failed of good** speech, {{Refs|11.511}}; γνώμης, ἐλπίδων, βουλήσεως ἁ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 498, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33, 92}}; ἁ. τοῦ χρησμοῦ **mistake** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71: with _accusative_, ἁ. τὸ ἀληθές 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139 (codices, τἀληθέος Schäfer).}}
**3** **fail of having, be deprived of,** mostly with _genitive_, χειρῶν ἐξ Ὀδυσῆος ἁμαρτήσεσθαι ὀπωπῆς **that I should lose** my sight by Ulysses' hands, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.512; τοῦ ῥυσίου θ᾽ ἥμαρτε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 535; ἁ. πιστῆς ἀλόχου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 879, compare 144:}} —once with _neuter_ _adjective_, οὐ γὰρ εἰκὸς.. ἐμὲ ὑμῶν ἁμαρτεῖν τοῦτό γ᾽ 'tis not seemly **that I should ask** this of you **in vain,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 231:—rare in Prose, ἡμάρτομεν τῆς Βοιωτίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.7.β,compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.50}}; δυοῖν κακοῖν οὐκ ἦν ἁμαρτεῖν (i.e. either one or the other) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.20, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1320:}} —so μηδὲ δυοῖν φθάσαι ἁμάρτωσιν, ἢ.. ἢ.. **fail** to be before-hand in one of two things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33.}}
**4** rarely, **fail to do, neglect,** φίλων ἡμάρτανε δώρων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.68; ξυμμαχίας ἁμαρτών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 213.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **do wrong, err, sin,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.501, 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.111, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 262, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1207, etc.; ἄκοντες ἡμαρτάνομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336e, compare 340e}}, etc.:—with _participle_, ἥμαρτε χρηστὰ μωμένη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1136; πρόθυμος ὢν ἥμαρτες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1630, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.1: with _dative_ _of things_, ἁ. ῥήματι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 489b; ἐν λόγοις [prev. author] “R.” 396a; τοιαῦθ᾽ ἁμαρτάνουσιν ἐν λόγοις ἔπη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1096:—with cognate _accusative_, ἁμαρτίαν ἁ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1249, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 320: with _neuter_}}_adjective_ or _pronoun_, αὐτὸς ἐγὼ τόδε γ᾽ ἤμβροτον I **erred** in this, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.154; πόλλ᾽ ἁμαρτών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 915; ἀνθρώπινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.40: in Prose more frequently ἁ. περί τινος}} or τι **do wrong in** a matter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 891e, “Phdr.” 242e; ἐπί τινι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.91 (codices)}}; ἁ. εἴς τινα **sin** against.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.138, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 968}}; ἐπὶ τὴν ἔλλειψιν, ἐπὶ τὸ πλεῖον, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1126b1, 1118b16; περί τινα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.7; τινί LXX.Judg.10.10.}}
**II.2** _passive_, ἡμαρτήθη ὁ ἐς Σικελίαν πλοῦς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, etc.: in}}_perfect_ _participle_, τἀμὰ δ᾽ ἡμαρτημένα my plans **are frustrate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 621; τῶν περὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα ἐς τὰ θεῖα ἁμαρτανομένων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 759c; ἀπειρίἁ αὐτὸ μᾶλλον ἢ ἀδικία ἡμαρτῆσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.5:—τὰ ἡμαρτημένα, τὰ ἁμαρτηθέντα, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 439, 1269, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.20.}}
**II.3** ἁμαρτανόμενος, as _adjective_, **wrong, mistaken,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 37d, +others; αἱ ἡμαρτημέναι πολιτεῖαι [prev. author] “R.” 449a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275b1, 1301a36}}; and of persons, ἡμαρτημένοι **mistaken,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1125a19.}}

266:
** ἁμαρτ-ία**, ἡ,
**a failure, fault,** τῶνδ᾽ ἁ. δόμων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1197, etc.; οὐ τῇ ἑαυτοῦ ἁμαρτίᾳ χρῆσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.4}}; ἁ. δόξης **error** of judgement, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32.}}
**2** in Philos. and Religion, **guilt, sin,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 660c, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1148a3, +others, LXX.Gen.18.20, +others, NT.John.8.46, +others}}

268:
** ἁμαρτωλ-ός**, όν,
**erroneous,** ἁμαρτωλότερον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1109a33}}; **erring,** ἐν πᾶσιν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.25c.}}
**2** **of bad character,** δοῦλοι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.73}} W.: with _genitive_, **sinning against,** θεῶν{{Refs|“Michel” 547.31 (from Telmessus):}} —ἁμαρτωλὴ γέρων, barbarism in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1111}}. _adverb_ **-ῶς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 24D.}}
**II** _substantive_ ἁμαρτωλός, ὁ, **sinner,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.13.13, +others, NT.Luke.18.13, +others}}

269:
** ἄμᾰχ-ος**, ον,
**without battle:** hence,
**I** **with whom no one fights, unconquerable,** of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.3, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 856 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 253, 1014 (Lyric poetry); χεῖρες 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).41; δύναμις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 240d, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.139}}: with _infinitive_, πολύποδες.. πᾶν ὅτι οὖν φαγεῖν ἄ.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.1, etc.}}: of places, **impregnable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84}}: of things, **irresistible,** κακόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.76; κῦμα θαλάσσης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 90: of feelings, ἄλγος [prev. author] “Ag.” 733; φθόνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 456}}; ἄ. πρᾶγμα, of a woman whose beauty is **irresistible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.36; ἄ. φιλοφροσύνη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.667d; ἄ. κάλλος 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.24; ἄ. τρυφή 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 16.23:}} —ἄμαχόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, like {ἀμήχανον}, 'tis **impossible** to do.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.13}}. _adverb_ **-ως** **irresistibly,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc. Cond.” 3}}; **incontestably,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.266.}}
**II** _active_, **not having fought, taking no part in the battle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.16}}; ἄ. διάγειν to remain **without** **fighting,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 4.4.9: ἄμαχον, τό}} **non-combatants,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 2.2, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 53.12; ἄ. νίκη}}**gained without fighting,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.472 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II.2** **disinclined to fight, not contentious,** {{NT|NT.1Tim.3.3, NT.Tit.3.2, compare “Inscription Cos” 325; ἄ. ἐβίωσα “Epigr Gr.” 387.6 (Apamea Cibotus).}}

271:
** ἀμέθυστος**, ον,
(μεθύω) **not drunken, without drunkenness,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.464c}}: _comparative_, {{Refs|“Geoponica” 7.34.2.}}
**II** **not intoxicating,** perhaps so used in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 19.133: as}}_substantive_, ἀμέθυστον, τό, **remedy against drunkenness,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.572, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.24}}; also ἀμέθυστος, ἡ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.15b, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.34c:}} —hence, such things as were supposed to act as remedies, _i.e. _,
**II.1** kind of **herb,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.647b}}; ={σέλινον ἄγριον}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.175.}}
**II.2** **amethyst,** {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|LXX.Exo.28.19, NT.Rev.21.20, 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 1122, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.204, 9.748 (5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Jun.)}}:—also ἀμέθυστον, τό, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).287 B 22 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3** ἀμέθυστον, τό, kind of **grape,** Colum.3.2.24.

272:
** ἀμελ-έω** [ᾰ],
_aorist_ ἠμέλησα, _Epic dialect_ ἀμ-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}}, see below: _perfect_ ἠμέληκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43}}: (ἀμελής):**—have no care for, be neglectful of,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always with _negative_ (not in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), οὐδ᾽ ὣς Μενελάου ἐφημοσύνης ἀμέλησεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.697; οὐκ ἀμέλησε κασιγνήτοιο πεσόντος 8.330}}; οὐκ ἀμέλησε Πατρόκλοιο πεσόντος **he lost** not **sight** of Patroclus [in order to **plunder** him],{{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.9:—after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, with and without _negative_, εἰ τούτων ἀμελήσει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.γ,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 989, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 900b, +others; δόξης ἀμελῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.227; ἀμελήσας ὑμῶν 21.167}}; (τούτῳ is _falsa lectio_ for{τούτου} in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 15); οὐκ ἐμοῦ τοῦ νομοθέτου ἀμελήσων ἀλλ᾽ εἰς τὸν Κρόνον αὐτόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 10.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **to be careless, negligent,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 400}}, frequently in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.78, etc.; τὸ μὴ ἀμελεῖν μάθε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 86; πῶς ἐπὶ τοῖς φθιμένοις ἀμελεῖν καλό; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 237.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.163}}: with _accusative_ _person_ et _participle_, **overlook:** hence, **allow, suffer,** παῖδας ἐκτεκνούμενος λάθρᾳ θνῄσκοντας ἀμελεῖ **lets** them die, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 439: with _genitive_, τοῦ ὀργίζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.9.}}
**4** with _infinitive_, **neglect** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98d, “Lg.” 944d, +others}}
**II** _passive_, **to be slighted, overlooked,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1094, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.68; ἐκφεύγει τἀμελούμενον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 111; οὐδ᾽ ἐκεῖνά μοι ἀμελεῖται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.2; οἱ ἠμελημένοι ἄνθρωποι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49.}}
**III** _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, **careless,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 8.7, 21.9}}. _adverb_ ἠμελημένως **carelessly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.19.}}
**IV** ἀμέλει, see entry

273:
** ἄμεμπτος**, ον,
**blameless, without reproach,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1158, “Cyc.” 342; ἀμέμπτους ὑμᾶς ἐδείξατε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.216}}; ἄ. χρόνου in regard of time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 692; ἄ. τἆλλα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 521; πρός τι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 629(uncertain); ἄ. ὑπὸ τῶν φίλων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.8}}; ἄ. ἐκείνῃ **without blame** to her, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 35}}: _comparative_ **-ότερος** **less blameworthy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 5.}}
**2** of things, **perfect in its kind,** δεῖπνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.2; δίκη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 945d; ἄ. πάντα ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.2}}. _adverb_ **-τως** **irreproachably,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 269, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1465, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.10, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.64.}}
**II** _active_, **not blaming, well content,** ἄμεμπτόν τινα ποιεῖν or ποιεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.52, 8.4.28.}}_adverb_ -τως, δέχεσθαί τινα{{Refs|[prev. work] 4.2.37.}}

274:
Included with: ** ἄμεμπτος**, ον,
**blameless, without reproach,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1158, “Cyc.” 342; ἀμέμπτους ὑμᾶς ἐδείξατε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.216}}; ἄ. χρόνου in regard of time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 692; ἄ. τἆλλα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 521; πρός τι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 629(uncertain); ἄ. ὑπὸ τῶν φίλων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.8}}; ἄ. ἐκείνῃ **without blame** to her, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 35}}: _comparative_ **-ότερος** **less blameworthy,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 5.}}
**2** of things, **perfect in its kind,** δεῖπνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.2; δίκη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 945d; ἄ. πάντα ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.2}}. _adverb_ **-τως** **irreproachably,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 269, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1465, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.10, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.64.}}
**II** _active_, **not blaming, well content,** ἄμεμπτόν τινα ποιεῖν or ποιεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.52, 8.4.28.}}_adverb_ -τως, δέχεσθαί τινα{{Refs|[prev. work] 4.2.37.}}

275:
** ἀμέριμν-ος**, ον,
**free from care, unconcerned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1083; βίος “Anthologia Graeca” 9.359 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus or 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus); ἀ. ὕπνον εὕδεις “Eranos” 13.87.}}_adverb_ -νως Vett. Val.355.6, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.5.7, “IG” 14.1839}}: _comparative_ -ότερον, ἔχειν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLips.” 105 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** _passive_, **uncared for,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1207.}}
**III** οἰνοχοεῖ κρήνης ἐξ ἀμεριμνοτέρης, either **causing less care,** i.e. **more easily attained,** of the fount of inspiration, or possibly **less celebrated,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 11.24 (Antip.).}}
**IV** ἀμέριμνον, τό, ={ἀείζωον μέγα}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 25.160.}}

276:
** ἀμετά-θετος**, ον,
**unalterable, immutable,** κατάληψις, of knowledge, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.20}}; of fate, Chrysipp.{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]2.264,compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.17.2; ἀκίνητα καὶ ἀ. “OGI” 331 (Pergam.)}}, etc. _adverb_ -τως, διακεῖσθαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.83, compare 6th c.AD: Asclepius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Metaphysicorum libros A-Z commentaria - pars” 22.6.}}
**2** Grammars, **not inflected,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 322.1.}}

278:
** ἀμετα-μέλητος**, ον,
**not to be repented of** or **regretted,** ἡδονή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 59d; τὸ πεπραγμένον αὐτοῖς ἀ. γίγνεται [prev. author] “Lg.” 866e}}; ἀμεταμέλητον ἐστί τί τινι one has **nothing to repent of,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.11.11.}}
**2** **having no opportunity of repentance,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 129.3.}}
**II** of persons, **unrepentant, feeling no remorse,** ἀ. ἀνίατος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1150a22, 1166a29}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 19.231a, “Inscription Prien.” 114.}}

279:
** ἀμετα-νόητος**, ον,
={ἀμεταμέλητος} 1, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 11, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.26, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 263.16, +others}}
**II** _active_, **unrepentant,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Rom.2.5, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 25}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PStrassb.” 29 (3rd c.AD).}}

280:
** ἄμετρος**, ον,
**without** or **beyond measure, immense,** κακόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.16 codices}}; opposed to μετρητός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 820c}}. _adverb_ **-τρως**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 86c, etc.}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 11.10.}}
**2** **immoderate,** in moral sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 690e, etc.}} _adverb_ **-τρως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.34.}}
**3** **neverceasing,** τέττιγες{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 174.}}
**4** **disproportionate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 87e}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος** **most unequal,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.7.}}
**II** **without metre, prosaic,** opposed to ἔμμετρος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451b1, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3, etc.}} _adverb_ **-τρως** **unmetrically,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 4}}; **not in metre,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.19.}}
**III** ἄμετρος, ={βάτος}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.37.}}

282:
** ἀμήτωρ**, ορος,
**motherless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 109, [prev. author] cited in Phot. p.91}} R.; of Melchizedek, {{NT|NT.Heb.7.3}}: of the number {{Refs 1st c.AD+|7, 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 20p.465M., compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.24; but ἡ μονὰς ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.934.}}
**II** **that is no mother, unmotherly,** μήτηρ ἀμήτωρ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1154}}; of dam mounted by her foal, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.261}}; **childless,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 365 (from Cotiaeum).}}

283:
** ἀμίαντος**, ον,
**undefiled, pure,** ὕδωρ{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 447; φάος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 142; αἰθήρ 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.86; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 578}} calls the sea ἡ ἀμίαντο; ἀ. τοῦ ἀνοσίου πέρι **free from stain** of ungodliness, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 777d; περὶ τῶν ὁσιωτάτων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 15.34; γάμοι οἱ ἀ. “epigram Gr.” 204.13 (from Cnidos), cf. NT.Heb.13.4; τόπος LXX.2Mac.15.34; κληρονομία NT.1Pet.1.4.}}
**2** **not to be defiled,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.75.}}
**II** ὁ ἀ. λίθος **asbestos,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 495, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.138, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 36.139.}}

285:
** ἄμμος**,
or ἅμμος (compare ὕφ-αμμος), ἡ,
**A** **sand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 110a, etc.}}
**A.II** **sandy ground, racecourse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.3.6. (Related to ἄμαθος as ψάμμος to ψάμαθος.)}}

287:
** ἀμοιβ-ή**, ἡ,
(ἀμείβω) **requital, recompense,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”; σοὶ δ᾽ ἄξιόν ἐστιν ἀμοιβῆς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.318; ἄλλοισι δίδου χαρίεσσαν ἀ... ἑκατόμβης 3.58; εὖ ἔρδοντι κακὴν ἀπέδωκας ἀ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1263, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 467; γλυκεῖαν μόχθων ἀ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.48}}; ἀγαναῖς ἀ. τινὰ τίνεσθαι to requite him **by like return,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 2.24; χαρίεσσα ἀμοιϝά “GDI” 3119c (from Corinth)}}; οἵας ἀ. ἐξ Ἰάσονος κυρεῖ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 23; ἀμοιβαὶ τῶν θυσιῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202e}}; **retribution,** ἔργων ἀντ᾽ ἀδίκων χαλεπὴν ἐπέθηκεν ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 334: _plural_, αἰωνίαις ἀ. βασανισθησόμενοι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.19.}}
**2** **repayment, compensation,** τείσουσι βοῶν ἐπιεικέ᾽ ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.382.}}
**3** **that which is given in exchange,** τῷ σκυτοτόμῳ ἀντὶ τῶν ὑποδημάτων ἀ. γίνεται κατ᾽ ἀξίαν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1163b35; τὴν ἀ. ποιητέον κατὰ τὴν προαίρεσιν 1164b1; δέκα μνῶν ἀ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 9.}}
**4** **answer,** ἀσχήμων ἐν τῇ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.160.}}
**II** **change, exchange,** τὰς ἀ. ποιεῖσθαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 11.4.4}}; of money, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 2.}}
**III** **change, alternation,** κακῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1147; ἑορτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 653d.}}
**III.2** **transformation,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.8.}}

288:
** ἄμπελος**, ἡ,
any climbing plant with tendrils, especially **grape-vine, Vitis vinifera** (ἄ. οἰνοφόρος{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.181}}; ἄ.τῆς Ἴδης is a variety, **V.see. apyrena, grape-currant,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.17.4), 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (not in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” except in _adjective_ ἀμπελόεις), etc.}}; πυροὶ καὶ κριθαὶ καὶ ἄμπελοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.110, compare 133, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.195, etc.; ἄ. καὶ ἐλάαν καὶ τὰ ἄλλα ἀκρόδρυα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.11}}; ἄ. τὴν περὶ τὸ ἱερὸν κόπτοντες, in collective sense (compare ἵππος, ἡ), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90}}; of wine, ἀμπέλου δρόσος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.2; ἀμπέλου παῖς [prev. author] “N.” 9.52.}}
**2** ἄ. ἀγρία **wild vine, Vitis silvestris,** [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.181, 5.2, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 23.19:—also ={ἄ. λευκή}, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus 9.14.1, 9.20.3, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.186.}}]
**3** ἄ. λευκή **bryony, Bryonia cretica,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.182, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.826 (but λευκὴ ἄ.}} white **grape,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.20.5).}}
**4** ἄ μέλαινα **black bryony, Tamus communis,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.183, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.827.}}
**5** ἄ. ποντία **wrack, Fucus volubilis,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.6.9.}}
**II** **vineyard,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.32.}}
**III** **engine for protecting besiegers, mantlet,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 141.7.}}
**IV** measure of length, = {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|20 παλαισταί, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Deff.” 131.}}
**V** ={αἰγιαλός} (Cyren.), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

292:
** ἀμύνω** [ῡ],
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἄμῡνον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.731:}}_future_ ἀμῠνῶ, _Ionic dialect_ **-ῠνέω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ -εῦσι [prev. work] 6}}: _aorist 1_ ἤμῡνα, _Epic dialect_ ἄμυνα [ᾰμ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.615}}: _aorist 2_, see at {ἀμυνάθω}:—_middle_, _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀμυνόμην {{Refs|[prev. work] 13.514}}: _future_ ἀμυνοῦμαι: _aorist 1_ ἠμυνάμην: _aorist 2_, see at {ἀμυνάθω}:—_passive_ rare (see. below c):—**keep off, ward off,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, mostly in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}} —Construction:
**1** with _accusative_ of the person or thing **to be kept off,** with _dative_ _person_ **for** or **from whom** danger averted, Δαναοῖσιν λοιγὸν ἄμυνον **ward off** ruin **from** the Danai, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.456, compare 341, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.525:— _dative_ frequently omitted, ὃς λοιγὸν ἀμύνει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.603; ἄ. τὸν βάρβαρον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 692e, compare “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 79}}. b. with _dative_ only, **defend, aid, succour,** ἀ. ὤρεσσι, σοῖσιν ἔτῃσι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.486, 6.262, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.500, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.87, 9.6, etc.}}; τοιαῦτ᾽ ἀμύνεθ᾽ Ἡρακλεῖ such **aid ye give** to H., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 219}}; ἀ. τῇ πόλει, τῷ δήμῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 577, 790; τῷ νόμῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 523, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67:}} —with _infinitive_ added, τοῖς μὲν οὐκ ἠμύνατε σωθῆναι so that they might be saved, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.80.}}
**2** with _accusative_ et _genitive_, Τρῶας ἄμυνε νεῶν **he kept** the Trojans **off from** the ships, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.731, compare 4.11, 12.402. b. with _genitive_ only, ἀ. νηῶν}} **defend** the ships, {{Refs|[prev. work] 13.109.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **succour,** χεῖρες ἀμύνειν hands **to aid,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 814; ἀμύνειν εἰσὶ καὶ ἄλλοι, are warded off,_ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.54}}; ἀμυνέσθω **let him be driven away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 845c.}}

293:
** ἀμφίβληστρ-ον**, τό,
**anything thrown round:**
**1** **casting-net,** {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 215, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141, 2.95; ἀμφιβλήστρῳ περιβάλλεσθαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 27, compare 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 7, 5th-6th c.BC(?): Epilycus Comicus 1, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 95, NT.Matt.4.18.}}
**1.b** _metaphorically_, of **the garment thrown like a net over** Agamemnon, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1382, “Ch.” 492}}; of the shirt of Nessus, Ἐρινύων ὑφαντὸν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1052}}; ἀμφίβληστρα σώματος ῥάκη rags **thrown around** body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1079.}}
**2** **fetter, bond,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 81.}}
**3** of **encircling** walls, ἀμφίβληστρα τοίχων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 96.}}

294:
** ἀμφιέννυμι**
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321a}}; **ἀμφιεννύω** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 9}}: _future_ ἀμφιέσω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.167, _Attic dialect_ ἀμφιῶ}} (ἀπ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 339}}, (προσ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 891}}: _aorist_ ἠμφίεσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.361 (_optative_ -έσαιμι), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.17}}:—_middle_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 8.2.21}}: _future_ **-έσομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.3.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 457a}}: _aorist_ ἠμφιεσάμην{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.122}}, _Epic dialect_ ἀμφιέσαντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.142}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ _participle_ ἀμφιεσθείς{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.10.5}}: _perfect_ ἠμφίεσμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1172, etc.}}; _poetry_ _participle_ ἀμφεμμένος{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 1035.25}}:— **put round** or **on,** ἀμφὶ δὲ καλὰ λέπαδν᾽ ἕσαν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.393}}: but mostly with _double_ _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, ἐμὲ χλαῖνάν τε χιτῶνά τε εἵματα.. ἀμφιέσασα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.369; in tmesi, ἀμφὶ δέ με χλαῖνάν τε χιτῶνά τε εἵματα ἕσσεν 10.542; ἀμφὶ δέ μιν μέγα δέρμα.. ἕσσ᾽ ἐλάφοιο 13.436, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 936, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.17, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἠμφιεσμένος τι **clothed in.., wearing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1172, “Th.” 92, “Ec.” 879, etc.}}; τροφαλὶς σκῖρον ἠμφιεσμένη **with** a rind **on,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 277.}}
**2** rarely with _dative_ _of things_. ἀ. τινά τινι **clothe** one **in** or **with,** θριξὶ καὶ δέρμασι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321a: _metaphorically_, πονηρὰ χρηστοῖς ἀ. λόγοις}} **cloak**.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.16.}}
**II** _middle_, **put on oneself, dress oneself in,** ἀμφιέσαντο χιτῶνας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.142; ἀμφὶ δ᾽ ἄρα.. ἑανὸν ἕσαθ᾽ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.178}}; ἀμφὶ δ᾽ ἄρα.. νεφέλην ὤμοισι ἕσαντο **they put** cloud **round their** shoulders,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|20.150; γυίοις ἀμφιέσαντο κόνιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta Elegiaca - PLG” 3; λευκήν ἀμφιέσασθε σάμην “Anthologia Graeca” 12.93; ἀρετὴν ἀντὶ ἱματίων ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 457a: _absolutely_, οὐ γὰρ παρέχεις ἀμφιέσασθαι τῷ πατρί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 17D.}}

295:
** ἀμφίπολις**,
_poetry_ ἀμφίπτολις, ὁ, ἡ, **encompassing city,** ἀνάγκη ἀμφίπτολις{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 75 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** _substantive_ ἀ., ἡ, **city encompassed by a river,** as _proper name_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.102, etc.}}

296:
** ἄμφοδον**, τό,
**street,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 327, “OGI” 483.80(Pergam.), NT.Mark.11.4, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.1}}; = **compitum,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**II** **block of houses surrounded by streets,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 137, “PMag.Par.” 1.349: proverbial, οὐ θύρᾳ ἀλλ᾽ ἀμφόδῳ διέψευσται 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.3.2}}: hence, **ward, quarter of a town,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Jer.17.27, “SIG” 961.1 (from Smyrna), 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 92.42, “BGU” 496 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.:—also ἄμφοδος, ἡ, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 16.1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Amos.5.16, 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Aedificiis” 2.3.}}

297:
** ἀμφότερος**
(_Locrian dialect_ ἀμφόταρος {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).334.39)}}, α, ον, (ἄμφω) rare in _singular_, **either,** i.e. **both of two** (opposed to ἑκάτερος **each one** of two), ἀμφοτέρας κοινὸν αἴας common to **either** land, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 131}}; ποίημα ἢ πάθος ἢ ἀμφότερον or **partaking of both,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 248d; τὸ ἀμφότερον ἑκατέρῳ οὐχ ἕπεται [prev. author] “Hp.Ma.” 302e.}}
**2** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has _sing._ only _neuter_ ἀμφότερον}} as _adverb_, followed by τε.. κα; ἀ. βασιλεύς τ᾽ ἀγαθὸς κρατερός τ᾽ αἰχμητής **both together,** prince as well as warrior, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.179}}; ἀ. γενεῇ τε καὶ οὕνεκα.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.60}}; followed by τε.. δέ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.79: also _neuter_ _plural_, ἀμφότερα μένειν πέμπειν τε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 480; φιλοχρήματος καὶ φιλότιμος, ἤτοι τὰ ἕτερα τούτων ἢ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 68c; ἀ. ἄριστος καὶ στρατηγὸς καὶ ῥαψῳδός “Ion” 541b}}; by τε.. καί.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.104.}}
**3** _dual_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, as ἀ. Αἴαντε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.265, +others}}, less frequently in later writers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 143c, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.134, etc.}}; but _plural_ is much more frequently, and is found with a _dual_ Noun, χεῖρε πετάσσας ἀμφοτέρας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.115.}} —Phrases: κατ᾽ ἀμφότερα **on both sides,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.β, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 159a; ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα}}**towards both sides, both ways,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.87, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.83, +others}}; ἀμφότερα, _absolutely_, **on both sides,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 13, +others}}; ἀπ᾽ ἀμφοτέρων **from** or **on both sides,** Ξέρξεω ἀπ᾽ ἀ. ἀδελφεός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.97; παρ᾽ ἀμφοτέρων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.7, +others}}; μετ᾽ ἀμφοτέροισι **one with another** (assuming variant), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 12.12}}; ἀμφοτέροις βλέπειν (i.e. ὄμμασι) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 32.6}}; ἀμφοτέραις, _Epic dialect_ -ῃσι (i.e. χερσί) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.264}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀμφοτέροις βεβακώς (i.e. ποσί) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.66.}}
**II** later, of more than two, **all together,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.19.16, “PLond.” 2.336.13 (2nd c.AD).}}

299:
** ἄμωμος**, ον,
**blameless,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 4; νόμος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.177}}; **without blemish,** εἶδος ἄ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 259; κάλλει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 185; in epitaphs, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 1974 (from Thessalonica), +others}}
**2** **unblemished,** of victims, etc., {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Exo.29.1, +others, NT.1Pet.1.19, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.171, +others}}
**3** **unimpaired, perfect,** ὑγίεια{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1119 (Geronthrae, 4th c.BC).}}
